WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Hello and welcome to mCoding where&nbsp;
the only limit is your imagination.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.081
And your download speed.

00:00:06.081 --> 00:00:08.018
But mostly your&nbsp;imagination.

00:00:08.018 --> 00:00:10.653
In this episode, we're talking 
about Python generators

00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:14.294
including the yield&nbsp;keyword, 
generator comprehensions, yield from.

00:00:14.294 --> 00:00:16.710
And how all 
this relates to async.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.354
You define a&nbsp;generator function in
 Python just like you would any other function

00:00:20.354 --> 00:00:23.354
using the def keyword and they&nbsp;
can take parameters.

00:00:23.354 --> 00:00:25.288
The only thing different that 
makes this a generator

00:00:25.288 --> 00:00:29.940
is the presence of one&nbsp;of these 
yield statements somewhere in the body.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.500
Generators act like normal functions. Except&nbsp;
when you hit a yield statement, it pauses.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.764
Every time you pause you 
can also yield&nbsp;a value,

00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:39.922
in this case, "hello" that 
becomes available to the caller.

00:00:39.922 --> 00:00:44.760
Unlike with functions,&nbsp;calling 
the generator doesn't run the generator.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.291
Instead printing out the generator, 
we just see&nbsp;a generator object.

00:00:49.291 --> 00:00:52.451
The way you actually run a 
generator is by calling its next method

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:55.418
which&nbsp;is what the next 
built-in will do.

00:00:55.418 --> 00:00:57.835
It will run the generator 
until it hits a yield statement.

00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:00.855
And&nbsp;return the value at 
the yield statement.

00:01:00.855 --> 00:01:04.752
With every next call, it resumes the
 generator until it hits&nbsp;another yield statement

00:01:04.752 --> 00:01:07.380
or until the function ends.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.849
If you resume the generator and the
 function&nbsp;ends before hitting another yield,

00:01:10.849 --> 00:01:14.335
you'll get a stop iteration 
exception raised.

00:01:14.335 --> 00:01:16.258
Returning a&nbsp;value from 
a generator is fine.

00:01:16.258 --> 00:01:20.812
But it doesn't appear as the 
result of a next call like the&nbsp;others do.

00:01:20.812 --> 00:01:22.942
The return value of the 
generator actually appears

00:01:22.942 --> 00:01:26.668
as an attribute on the stop&nbsp;
iteration exception that's raised.

00:01:26.668 --> 00:01:30.427
This is mainly for a very niche purpose
 that we'll talk about at&nbsp;the end.

00:01:30.427 --> 00:01:35.563
While Ariana Grande might prefer to thank you 
next next next over and over again,

00:01:35.563 --> 00:01:39.600
in Python,&nbsp;it's more common to 
let a for-loop do it for you.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.723
This works because under the hood a for-loop&nbsp;will 
call that next function over and over&nbsp;again

00:01:43.723 --> 00:01:47.394
until it finds a stop iteration 
at which&nbsp;point it stops.

00:01:47.394 --> 00:01:50.720
Check out my video on what 
a for-loop actually translates to under the hood

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.701
if you&nbsp;haven't seen it already.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:57.323
In any case, here we see the items printed out hello world123.

00:01:57.323 --> 00:01:59.819
So what are&nbsp;generators good for?

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:04.983
The most common use case for a
 generator is to define an iterator of a class.

00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:08.100
For a simple example, 
consider this `Range` class.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.173
Just pretend you don't know 
about the built-in&nbsp;range.

00:02:10.173 --> 00:02:12.600
And you're building 
a Range class yourself.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.637
In the spirit of being lazy 
like a generator,

00:02:14.637 --> 00:02:17.546
our ranges are all going to 
go from zero to some stop value.

00:02:17.546 --> 00:02:19.937
We don't support start or&nbsp;step.

00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:23.100
So just like the built-in range which
 we don't know exists,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.580
range of 5 is the numbers&nbsp;0, 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.975
But when we create the Range, we don't
 actually store all those numbers&nbsp;somewhere.

00:02:29.975 --> 00:02:32.400
We just store the start on the stop.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.897
But if all we have is a 
start and a stop,

00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:39.043
how do we&nbsp;iterate over the elements of
 the Range that we're supposed to be representing?

00:02:39.043 --> 00:02:43.416
The answer is the&nbsp;highly 
sophisticated solution of counting.

00:02:43.416 --> 00:02:45.179
Start at the starting value.

00:02:45.179 --> 00:02:50.880
And then continually yield the&nbsp;
current value and add 1 until we get to the stop.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.380
We can now iterate over our Range just the&nbsp;same
 as we would over the built-in range.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.022
And just like the built-in range because we're&nbsp;
only storing the start and the stop,

00:02:59.022 --> 00:03:03.434
not all the numbers in between, we 
can construct huge&nbsp;ranges.

00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:09.049
There's no way a list of numbers from 0 all 
the way up to this number would ever fit&nbsp;into memory.

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:13.346
Yet we can construct the Range 
and iterate over it quickly and efficiently.

00:03:13.346 --> 00:03:17.234
And to&nbsp;reiterate it's this laziness where
 we're not actually constructing those numbers

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:21.060
until we ask&nbsp;to see them 
that allows us to be able to do this.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.329
So, while generators can be slightly slower&nbsp;
than lists in certain situations,

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:27.208
if you're just processing them 
one at a time

00:03:27.208 --> 00:03:29.473
like here we're&nbsp;
just printing out the current n,

00:03:29.473 --> 00:03:32.553
then a generator can be 
a huge win over a list.

00:03:32.553 --> 00:03:34.158
And here's a little&nbsp;history for you.

00:03:34.158 --> 00:03:38.111
The built-in range in Python 2 actually
 used to return a list.

00:03:38.111 --> 00:03:39.600
That turned out to&nbsp;be 
a huge mistake.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.360
And for Python 3, it was changed to be
 something more similar to what the generator&nbsp;is doing.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.659
It's not actually using a generator. 
It's using something handwritten in C.

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:49.785
But it's&nbsp;the same idea.

00:03:49.785 --> 00:03:54.479
Another very common and useful place that 
you might use the generator is reading&nbsp;from a file.

00:03:54.479 --> 00:03:58.400
Once again, this is a situation where
 because files can be so big,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.160
you might not want&nbsp;to read 
the whole file into memory all at once.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.813
If you can process things line 
by line using a&nbsp;generator,

00:04:04.813 --> 00:04:08.494
then even if the file is gigabytes big, 
it doesn't matter.

00:04:08.494 --> 00:04:12.525
You'll only need as much memory&nbsp;
as you would need to process a single line.

00:04:12.525 --> 00:04:15.385
So for example, I have 
some custom dataclass.

00:04:15.385 --> 00:04:17.633
In this case,&nbsp;it's just xyz points.

00:04:17.633 --> 00:04:19.047
My file just looks like this.

00:04:19.047 --> 00:04:23.267
I just have floating point 
values xyx, xyz, xyz.

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:25.071
I don't know what's up with 
the red squiggle.

00:04:25.071 --> 00:04:27.935
pycharm thinks I have a 
syntax error in my text&nbsp;file.

00:04:27.935 --> 00:04:31.006
Feel free to free associate about 
what the problem is.

00:04:31.006 --> 00:04:34.560
In any case, we define a generator 
that&nbsp;expects a file handle.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.336
What I mean by that is, the file 
object that you get back from an open call.

00:04:38.336 --> 00:04:43.432
In a very generator like fashion,
 iterating over a file actually iterates over the lines of the file.

00:04:43.432 --> 00:04:46.850
We strip off the trailing new line and
 split it by the commas.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.782
Then we convert everything to floats.

00:04:48.782 --> 00:04:51.809
Create one of our custom data 
structures and yield it.

00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:56.280
And then we just print out the rows. Of course,&nbsp;you 
can do whatever data processing you like.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.906
The next very common use case for generators is&nbsp;
to think about them as lazy sequences.

00:05:00.906 --> 00:05:03.131
You can loop over them 
repeatedly returning values.

00:05:03.131 --> 00:05:06.331
So you&nbsp;can think about those as 
values of some sequence,

00:05:06.331 --> 00:05:10.965
whether it be a mathematical sequence like in this&nbsp;
case, or just a sequence of objects

00:05:10.965 --> 00:05:14.667
And you just don't compute the next
 term in the sequence&nbsp;until someone asks for it.

00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:16.907
So, here's a `collatz` sequence.

00:05:16.907 --> 00:05:19.183
Take a positive integer n.

00:05:19.183 --> 00:05:21.216
If it's even,&nbsp;divided by 2.

00:05:21.216 --> 00:05:23.700
Otherwise, multiply by 3 and add 1.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.088
Then repeat.

00:05:25.088 --> 00:05:27.691
If you ever got to 1 then the sequence&nbsp;
would start repeating,

00:05:27.691 --> 00:05:30.869
it would start to go 1, 4, 2, 1, 4, 2 and so on.

00:05:30.869 --> 00:05:35.574
As of 2022, it's&nbsp;one of the world's 
most famous unsolved problems in math.

00:05:35.574 --> 00:05:38.721
Starting at any number do 
you always get&nbsp;to 1?

00:05:38.721 --> 00:05:41.487
Or could there be a sequence 
that goes off to infinity?

00:05:41.487 --> 00:05:45.049
Or maybe some other cycle 
like 4, 2, 1, 4, 2, 1?

00:05:45.049 --> 00:05:48.527
Well I'm here to announce that 
I've actually proved the Collatz Conjecture

00:05:48.527 --> 00:05:52.320
to be independent of 
the axioms of mathematics.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.382
Just kidding.

00:05:53.382 --> 00:05:57.540
Anyway, here's what a&nbsp;
typical Collatz sequence looks like.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.113
It does some unpredictable stuff.

00:05:59.113 --> 00:06:02.799
And then&nbsp;eventually, you hit 
a power of 2 and it shrinks down to 1.

00:06:02.799 --> 00:06:05.796
This showcases another very 
important&nbsp;property of generators.

00:06:05.796 --> 00:06:09.030
Imagine, if instead of a 
generator we were using a list.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:12.139
Well, besides the&nbsp;fact that this 
list might need to be arbitrarily large

00:06:12.139 --> 00:06:14.731
because we don't know 
how long a Collatz sequence will be,

00:06:14.731 --> 00:06:16.702
what if I didn't care about 
the whole sequence?

00:06:16.702 --> 00:06:19.265
For instance, what if I just 
wanted&nbsp;to know how long is it?

00:06:19.265 --> 00:06:21.400
If you're wondering, it's 111 elements.

00:06:21.401 --> 00:06:25.032
But if I return to list in this case,&nbsp;
that would have been a huge waste.

00:06:25.032 --> 00:06:28.618
Why allocate all that memory and 
store all those numbers just to&nbsp;get the length?

00:06:28.618 --> 00:06:31.053
If I wanted to be more efficient, I'd 
need to write another function

00:06:31.053 --> 00:06:33.491
that calculates&nbsp;the 
length instead of storing the list.

00:06:33.491 --> 00:06:36.867
But that length function would be
 basically identical to&nbsp;the list function.

00:06:36.867 --> 00:06:40.883
Just instead of appending into a list,
 we add one to account.

00:06:40.883 --> 00:06:44.777
if only there was&nbsp;a way to have one
 implementation of the Collatz sequence

00:06:44.777 --> 00:06:46.760
that I can do whatever I want with.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.135
Once&nbsp;again, generators to the rescue.

00:06:49.135 --> 00:06:53.300
If I want the length of the sequence, then 
I just count one for&nbsp;every element of the sequence.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.373
Once again, we see 111.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:59.860
And if I did actually want the whole list of&nbsp;
numbers in memory, then I can just call list on it.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.767
Generators can even be used to represent 
sequences&nbsp;that we know are infinite.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:06.352
We can only ever use finitely 
many terms.

00:07:06.352 --> 00:07:10.407
But we're able to compute as&nbsp;many 
as we need without specifying ahead of time.

00:07:10.407 --> 00:07:14.902
So, you could represent all the powers of two all&nbsp;the
 rational numbers of Fibonacci Sequence

00:07:14.902 --> 00:07:16.710
or all the prime numbers.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.372
All you need is an algorithm&nbsp;
for enumerating them.

00:07:19.372 --> 00:07:22.657
Defining a generator is as simple as
 defining a regular function.

00:07:22.657 --> 00:07:25.846
But you can&nbsp;go even simpler 
if your generator is simple enough.

00:07:25.846 --> 00:07:28.990
This is a list comprehension which
 hopefully&nbsp;you're familiar with.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.472
And it creates a list whose elements are
 x times x for each x in the&nbsp;range.

00:07:33.472 --> 00:07:38.306
Replace those brackets with parentheses. 
And you now have a generator comprehension.

00:07:38.306 --> 00:07:42.293
This&nbsp;is really just shorthand notation
 for defining and calling a generator function.

00:07:42.293 --> 00:07:45.416
Meaning this&nbsp;code and the elements
 of this sequence will not be computed

00:07:45.416 --> 00:07:48.239
until you try to actually iterate&nbsp;
over the generator.

00:07:48.239 --> 00:07:50.907
And once again this can be 
 more efficient than the list version

00:07:50.907 --> 00:07:53.947
which creates&nbsp;all the 
elements in memory immediately.

00:07:53.947 --> 00:07:56.702
If you happen to be immediately passing 
a generator&nbsp;into a function,

00:07:56.702 --> 00:07:58.413
you can also do it this way.

00:07:58.413 --> 00:08:00.457
This creates a generator just 
like in the previous&nbsp;line.

00:08:00.457 --> 00:08:02.559
And passes it to the sum function.

00:08:02.559 --> 00:08:05.060
Basically, it just lets you leave 
off a pair of parentheses.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.880
And&nbsp;now that we know about 
generator comprehensions,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.190
another great feature of generators is
 that&nbsp;they're extremely easy to compose.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:16.629
You can build pipelines of data
 out of generators in no time.

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:19.445
Suppose, you want to be 
able to parse a file like this.

00:08:19.445 --> 00:08:22.786
It has data in it that you want 
to treat&nbsp;as floating point numbers.

00:08:22.786 --> 00:08:26.381
But you also want to allow 
comments, full-line comments

00:08:26.381 --> 00:08:30.183
nans, infinities&nbsp;and blank lines.

00:08:30.183 --> 00:08:31.444
No need to write a fancy parser.

00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:34.679
Generators are plenty expressive
 enough to get&nbsp;this job done.

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.295
We start by opening the file and
 iterating over the rows.

00:08:37.295 --> 00:08:39.907
Remember each row is&nbsp;
one line of the file.

00:08:39.907 --> 00:08:41.368
Strip off the new line.

00:08:41.368 --> 00:08:44.731
And remove anything after a hash in 
order to strip&nbsp;trailing comments.

00:08:44.731 --> 00:08:48.664
Then Define another generator that
 loops over the generator from the first line.

00:08:48.664 --> 00:08:51.000
All it does is filter out empty lines. 

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.881
Each lines should now contain
 a floating point number.

00:08:52.881 --> 00:08:55.675
So,&nbsp;we use float to convert it 
from a string to a float.

00:08:55.675 --> 00:08:59.759
Then we do another filter operation to&nbsp;
throw out any Infinities or nans.

00:08:59.759 --> 00:09:03.502
Then let's just pretend that 
we want to replace anything&nbsp;negative with 0.

00:09:03.502 --> 00:09:07.311
And just for something to do,
 let's just say we want to add up those numbers.

00:09:07.311 --> 00:09:08.452
This was very simple to write

00:09:08.452 --> 00:09:12.859
and easy to read like a step-by-step instruction 
manual on&nbsp;how to create the pipeline.

00:09:12.859 --> 00:09:17.177
And once again all this happens lazily.
 So, it's very memory efficient.

00:09:17.177 --> 00:09:21.519
We've completely defined our pipeline
 before we ever actually read from the file.

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:25.576
At this line&nbsp;in the code, we haven't 
even read a single byte from the file.

00:09:25.576 --> 00:09:30.527
Each next call inside the sum&nbsp;triggers
 this generator to look for one more element.

00:09:30.527 --> 00:09:33.235
That triggers this generator to compute&nbsp;
one more element

00:09:33.235 --> 00:09:35.503
which triggers this generator 
to compute more elements

00:09:35.503 --> 00:09:37.426
until it finds a finite one.

00:09:37.426 --> 00:09:40.868
Which triggers this one to compute
 more elements until it finds a non-empty row.

00:09:40.868 --> 00:09:44.629
Which triggers this&nbsp;one 
which finally reads a line from the file.

00:09:44.629 --> 00:09:46.182
So, we're able to process
 the whole file.

00:09:46.182 --> 00:09:49.500
And we don't&nbsp;even need more 
than one line at a time in memory.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.808
And now we get to the advanced 
usage of generators.

00:09:52.808 --> 00:09:55.409
A yield statement is not 
just a statement.

00:09:55.409 --> 00:09:59.311
It's also an expression. 
It returns a value back to&nbsp;you.

00:09:59.311 --> 00:10:03.616
And that's because generators are 
not just possible functions that yield values.

00:10:03.616 --> 00:10:06.507
Generators&nbsp;are 
actually bi-directional pipelines.

00:10:06.507 --> 00:10:09.491
Just like a generator can yield 
a value up to its caller,

00:10:09.491 --> 00:10:12.887
Its&nbsp;caller can send a value 
back down to the generator.

00:10:12.887 --> 00:10:16.473
And it's these sent values that 
are returned&nbsp;from the yield expression.

00:10:16.473 --> 00:10:18.157
So, here's how we read this.

00:10:18.157 --> 00:10:19.885
We have a worker generator.

00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:22.966
The worker has a&nbsp;collection 
of tasks, initially empty.

00:10:22.966 --> 00:10:27.050
Initially, we yield none because we
 haven't had any chance to&nbsp;receive any tasks.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:30.357
Our caller is expected to send 
us a batch of tasks.

00:10:30.357 --> 00:10:33.725
The idea being that we're&nbsp;
supposed to evaluate the given function

00:10:33.725 --> 00:10:36.367
using the arguments that 
the caller passed to us.

00:10:36.367 --> 00:10:41.196
If the&nbsp;caller passes some new tasks,
 then we extend our task list with those arguments.

00:10:41.196 --> 00:10:45.120
Otherwise, we assume&nbsp;that
 the caller is asking us to complete a task.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.000
So, if there's a task available, 
we pop it off.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.125
Evaluate the function with those 
arguments to get some value.

00:10:51.125 --> 00:10:53.364
which then gets yielded 
back out&nbsp;to the caller.

00:10:53.364 --> 00:10:55.780
And here's how a caller would 
use the worker.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:59.424
So, our worker is just going to convert&nbsp;
whatever arguments we give it to a string.

00:10:59.424 --> 00:11:02.388
We use the send method to send
 values into the generator.

00:11:02.388 --> 00:11:06.000
However, when we just create the
 generator it doesn't start running it.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.469
The very first value&nbsp;that we send 
can't possibly be accepted by the worker

00:11:09.469 --> 00:11:11.337
because the function is going 
to start at&nbsp;the beginning.

00:11:11.337 --> 00:11:13.121
There's no yield statement there.

00:11:13.121 --> 00:11:17.349
So, first we just send none to 
cause the generator to&nbsp;run to the first yield.

00:11:17.349 --> 00:11:20.306
Just like a call to next, 
send will cause the generator to run

00:11:20.306 --> 00:11:22.731
until yield&nbsp;or until the 
function returns.

00:11:22.731 --> 00:11:25.929
After the send none,
 the generator will be paused here.

00:11:25.929 --> 00:11:29.868
So now, we'll&nbsp;send in three tasks: 
the number 1, the number 2, and the number 3.

00:11:29.868 --> 00:11:31.703
These are wrapped as single&nbsp;
element tuples

00:11:31.703 --> 00:11:34.462
because we're processing them 
using star args.

00:11:34.462 --> 00:11:38.501
Now, if we call next three times, 
then&nbsp;we'll see our three values evaluated.

00:11:38.501 --> 00:11:41.956
When use the next call, 
the return value from yield is going&nbsp;to be none.

00:11:41.956 --> 00:11:44.689
We can send in more values 
and then print them out.

00:11:44.689 --> 00:11:47.234
And that's part of the 
usefulness&nbsp;of this setup.

00:11:47.234 --> 00:11:50.562
I can add tasks or evaluate 
tasks at any time.

00:11:50.562 --> 00:11:52.994
I don't need to have everything 
prepared&nbsp;ahead of time.

00:11:52.994 --> 00:11:55.567
And I don't need to compute 
all the answers at once.

00:11:55.567 --> 00:11:59.597
And another thing you can do is, use the 
throw method to throw an exception inside the generator.

00:11:59.597 --> 00:12:04.349
As you can see, the exception acts&nbsp;
as if it was thrown from the yield statement.

00:12:04.349 --> 00:12:08.108
So, you could surround this in a try-except if you&nbsp;
wanted to handle exceptions that way.

00:12:08.108 --> 00:12:11.009
There's also the close method that 
does basically the&nbsp;same thing as throw

00:12:11.009 --> 00:12:14.645
except it throws a 
special generator exit exception.

00:12:14.645 --> 00:12:16.250
This exception gets&nbsp;special treatment.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:21.015
And it's basically a way for you to cancel 
the generator without having that&nbsp;error propagate up.

00:12:21.015 --> 00:12:25.553
So at this point, this should feel 
very familiar to something else in Python.

00:12:25.553 --> 00:12:28.629
We're basically submitting tasks 
into this worker thing.

00:12:28.629 --> 00:12:30.021
And then something drives 
the worker.

00:12:30.021 --> 00:12:32.254
And&nbsp;the worker decides how 
the tasks are scheduled

00:12:32.254 --> 00:12:34.842
and when to actually call the 
function and do&nbsp;the work.

00:12:34.842 --> 00:12:37.620
Doesn't that sound 
a lot like async?

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:40.267
I don't expect you to already know 
all about async.

00:12:40.267 --> 00:12:42.681
Don't worry, I've got 
a video on that coming.

00:12:42.681 --> 00:12:46.188
But just the general idea of 
defining tasks and&nbsp;pausing functions

00:12:46.188 --> 00:12:48.611
and continuing later when 
things are convenient.

00:12:48.611 --> 00:12:50.316
Well, it's no coincidence.

00:12:50.316 --> 00:12:56.644
As&nbsp;it happens, under the hood in Python, async 
await co-routines are defined in terms of generators.

00:12:56.644 --> 00:12:59.790
So, once again, being lazy is 
paying off big time.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:05.220
The lazy machinery of generators is powerful&nbsp;
enough to design an entire async framework around.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.076
And that's not even the 
end of the video

00:13:07.076 --> 00:13:10.643
because&nbsp;we still have one 
major feature of generators left to cover.

00:13:10.643 --> 00:13:12.224
`yield from`

00:13:12.224 --> 00:13:17.400
`yield from` allows one&nbsp;generator 
to yield values from another generator.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:23.420
In most cases, you could use it exactly like you&nbsp;
would a for-loop looping over the values and&nbsp;yielding them.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.451
And that's totally fine. 
There's&nbsp;nothing wrong with doing that.

00:13:26.451 --> 00:13:28.311
Using `yield from` is going to 
be one line shorter.

00:13:28.311 --> 00:13:31.813
And it's going&nbsp;to avoid 
using an extra local variable.

00:13:31.813 --> 00:13:35.000
However, that's not the intended 
use of `yield from`.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.520
And&nbsp;it's not why it was introduced 
into the language.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:39.735
Just think about it.

00:13:39.735 --> 00:13:43.521
Do you think they would really&nbsp;
introduce a whole new set of keywords

00:13:43.521 --> 00:13:46.531
just to have a shortened for-loop.

00:13:46.531 --> 00:13:49.964
The true purpose of `yield&nbsp;from` 
was that it was introduced into the language

00:13:49.964 --> 00:13:54.383
in order to facilitate the 
bi-directional nature&nbsp;of generators.

00:13:54.383 --> 00:13:56.905
Remember a caller can receive 
values from generators.

00:13:56.905 --> 00:14:00.242
But they can also send values 
to&nbsp;generators.

00:14:00.242 --> 00:14:04.448
But what if a generator wants to take
 the values that it receives from its caller

00:14:04.448 --> 00:14:07.168
and&nbsp;pass them to a sub-generator.

00:14:07.168 --> 00:14:09.955
For instance, here's a quiet worker.

00:14:09.955 --> 00:14:12.293
It's another generator because it&nbsp;
has a yield.

00:14:12.293 --> 00:14:14.558
And all it does is it creates 
a worker.

00:14:14.558 --> 00:14:20.279
And then yield from allows the worker to pass&nbsp;
messages from its caller directly into the worker.

00:14:20.279 --> 00:14:24.269
And likewise whatever the worker 
yields is yielded&nbsp;back up to the caller.

00:14:24.269 --> 00:14:29.106
If a task causes an error, 
the quiet worker just catches it.

00:14:29.106 --> 00:14:31.462
And then&nbsp;creates a new worker 
to keep going.

00:14:31.462 --> 00:14:33.339
This is of course very bad practice

00:14:33.339 --> 00:14:37.080
because the task&nbsp;queue of 
the worker may not have been empty.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.526
But we're throwing it out and creating 
a new&nbsp;worker anyway.

00:14:40.526 --> 00:14:45.347
In any case, the `yield from` is what allows
us to pass messages bi-directionally.

00:14:45.347 --> 00:14:47.427
It essentially acts as a pass-through.

00:14:47.427 --> 00:14:50.606
Taking whatever messages from our
 caller and passing&nbsp;them to the worker.

00:14:50.606 --> 00:14:54.993
And taking whatever messages from
 our worker and yielding them up to the&nbsp;caller.

00:14:54.993 --> 00:14:57.720
That is the true purpose of `yield from`.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:02.746
The fact that you can use them to write one 
line&nbsp;shorter for-loops is just a bonus.

00:15:02.746 --> 00:15:05.221
Oh yeah. And just like yield, 
`yield from` also returns something.

00:15:05.221 --> 00:15:07.680
it's the return value of 
the subgenerator.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:10.449
That thing inside the stop iteration from 
the&nbsp;beginning of the video.

00:15:10.449 --> 00:15:12.960
This is its real purpose.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.963
So, there you have it. 
That's all I've got on&nbsp;generators.

00:15:15.963 --> 00:15:18.176
I hope you learned something. 
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:15:18.176 --> 00:15:22.080
Let me know in the comments&nbsp;how
 much you want me to make that async video.

